Dear Mr. Psycho, & list:

The W2K beta that is most widely available is the RC1, build 2072.  It
includes FX!32 as an integral part of the OS (I'm not sure if "kernel"
means much in the MS context).  Build 2072 includes all the server
stuff.  It is a pretty decent OS and will run a lot of 32-bit windows
software.  In fact, if you can install it, it will probably run (except
for drivers).  I ran my CAD program and ArcView 3.2 GIS (but not ArcGis
8.xx) .   I was able to run Riven, sort of, including the sound.

RC2, I found it once on the net and downloaded but it was corrupt.  I'm
not sure what the difference is.

That said, I'm not certain it is superior to NT 4.0.  NT runs startling
quick on late-model Alpha hardware.  A lot of the drivers that are
still kicking around will load under NT but not under 2K.

I got my copy I won't tell where, and tried to pay MS for it.  I sent a
$20 check to Redmond, but it never got cashed.  I tried to contribute
to the Bill & Melinda foundation but they wouldn't take my $ either.
Given that, I would say the OS is freeware.

It is pretty stable, and I would be surprised if many of the virii and
trojans loose on the net would run in 64-bit mode.   I have in the past
run both NT and W2K in dual boot configurations with Tru64.  The only
issue is that each OS whacks the clock differently, so you spend a lot
of time going from 1984 to 2052 and back.

It's too bad Compaq pulled the plug on Windows Alpha, just when it was
starting to take off.  It could have kept Alp[ha alive.  Just imagine a
parallel universe where the power users are clamoring for the latest
6Ghz, dual core EV97, but the smart guys are waiting for next year's
release of the 128-bit EVX with its discorporate tunnel memory that
somehow retrieves information from what 20th century thinkers called
"the future."

later,

jn



