O.k. ...

About the HALT button:
It's not a momentary like it is on a PC, it's push-it's on, push-it's
off kind of "latching" button, rather like PC's had for power before all
this power management stuff evolved.

About your other problem:
I saw that booting up OpenVMS while using the serial console. Turns out
the SRM "console" variable was set to "GRAPHICS", it needed to be
"SERIAL". Your post doesn't mention whether you're using the graphics
console and a keyboard and mouse or if you are using the serial console
port, so dunno if that helps. Get to the console prompt and see what
this command returns:

P00>>> show console

If it says "serial and you're using a monitor, keyboard and mouse
connected directly to the Alpha, enter these commands and then try to
boot (after correcting the HALT button thing):

P00>>> set console graphics

P00>>> init

Likewise, if you're using the serial console port and the show command
returns graphics:

P00>>> set console serial

P00>>> init

...the try to boot.

I'm a VMS guy, so I'll have to leave it at that - never booted a
UN*X-like o.s. on an Alpha except for some distant experiences I barely
remember (Tru64).

